{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel saw the face of Moshe.</b> It is fitting that you be roused here to ask why Scripture mentions Moshe three times, when it could have said simply that the skin of his face radiated and he restored the veil. By way of the plain sense (peshat), one may explain that this corresponds to the three periods of forty days that he stood on the mountain. It is known that in each standing there was added to him an increase of attainment and an addition of light; therefore the section mentions three radiations, for splendor and majesty were cast upon him in each forty days — the first, the second, and the third. And the allusion comes here in this section through three radiations and in this verse through three times 'Moshe' and three times 'faces' (פנים), because now with the second tablets the third forty days were completed. And Scripture mentioned Moshe three times to indicate that in each and every instance he ascended a level worthy of being called on it by the name Moshe, for the word Moshe is derived from כי מן המים משיתיהו, and what happened in his childhood — that he was drawn from the waters — was an allusion to what would happen in his old age: that he would be drawn from the waters of wisdom at Mount Sinai. Therefore they said: 'water is nothing but Torah.' And in his name too there is a rousing and an allusion to what he was destined to hear — the voice of the speech of the Shekhinah from between the two keruvim." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, one may say that the reason this verse mentions three times 'Moshe' and three times 'faces' (פנים) is to explain the greatness of Moshe on the mountain, for he was exalted in that holy station and his glory was likened to the glory of the Holy One, blessed be He. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moshe, 'How much glory have I granted you! You said: קומה ה׳, קומה ה׳ — Arise, O YHWH; שובה ה׳, שובה ה׳ — Return, O YHWH. The Torah is called by My name, as it is said (Psalms 19:8): ״תורת ה׳ תמימה״; I called it by your name, as it is said (Malachi 3:22): ״זכרו תורת משה עבדי״. Israel is called by My name, as it is said (Psalms 81): ״לו עמי שומע לי״; I said they would be called by your name (Isaiah 63:11): ״ויזכור ימי עולם משה עמו״. I spoke with you in saying (אמירה) and in speech (דבור), as it is said: וידבר ה׳ אל משה לאמר, ויאמר ה׳ אל משה לאמר; and you too spoke before Me in speech and in saying, as it is said (Numbers 11:11): ״ויאמר משה אל ה׳״, (Exodus 6:8–9): ״וידבר משה אל ה׳״. As for Me, there is before Me no eating and drinking; I made you like Me, as it is said (Exodus 34): ״לחם לא אכל ומים לא שתה״. I am Elohim, and you I appointed as Elohim, as it is said (Exodus 7): ״ראה נתתיך אלהים לפרעה״. To Me belong prophets, and to you I gave a prophet, as it is said (ibid.): ״ואהרן אחיך יהיה נביאך״. As for Me, no creature can look upon Me, as it is said (Exodus 33): ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״; and as for you, likewise, as it is said (Exodus 30): ״וייראו מגשת אליו״. Of Me it is written (Exodus 33): ״וראית את אחורי״, and of you it is written: והביטו אחרי משה — end of the Midrash." "<b>And it seems</b> to me that the masters of truth in this Midrash spoke by way of generality and brevity concerning the excellences of Moshe through which he was exalted and likened to the Holy One, blessed be He. The particulars are many; we can understand the particular from within the general. And so it would have been possible to say: 'As for Me, I have an attendant called נער (na'ar), and you have an attendant called נער,' as it is said (ibid.): ״ומשרתו יהושע בן נון נער״. And because of this matter Yehoshua was perforce called נער, though he was fifty-six years old. And likewise one could say further: 'As for Me, I have seventy princes above whose power derives from My power, and you have seventy elders whose power derives from your power,' as it is said (Numbers 11:25): ״ויאצל מן הרוח אשר עליו ויתן על שבעים איש הזקנים״; and just as the seventy princes above surround My throne, so it is written (ibid. 11): ״ויעמד אותם סביבות האהל״. <br><b>And after</b> we have observed how this Midrash revealed to us the glory of Moshe and how it likened his face to the faces above, I shall now inform you and teach you wisdom in the reason that Scripture mentioned 'faces' three times. It is known to the understanding that there are ten levels, divided into three levels of three each, for the first is called אין (ayin) and has no mention; and the first level is called כבוד עליון (supreme glory), and the second level is called כבוד אמצעי (middle glory), and the third level is called כבוד אחרון (last glory). And this is the secret of the Mishnah in the commandment of shofar-blowing — the order of blasts, three by three. And all the levels collectively are called כבוד (glory) and פנים (face). And this is what is written: לא תוכל לראות את פני — this is the first glory; ופני לא יראו — this is the middle glory; פני ילכו — this is the last glory. And even though Moshe attained the last glory and also the middle one, we nevertheless find concerning him: just as the Holy One, blessed be He, wrote of Himself three times, so He wrote in this verse three times 'Moshe' and three times 'faces.' <br><b>From now</b> on, observe the greatness of the prophet, upon him be peace, whom Scripture likened to his Creator — a thing no one born of woman attained to this level. And for this reason too He mentioned 'veil' (מסוה) three times: two of them are withholding and one is attainment. And understand this." "<b>Until they came to speak with him.</b> It should have said 'until they came to speak with YHWH,' and Scripture repeats the name of Moshe many times and does not substitute it. Why does it now substitute the particular Name [i.e., refer to Moshe by pronoun] in the place where it should have mentioned it [i.e., YHWH] and does not mention it? Rather, one may say that it is all elevation and glory for Moshe, so that you may understand from this as well: 'until YHWH came to speak with Moshe' — and Scripture teaches you that Moshe comes to speak to YHWH and YHWH to Moshe, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 33:11): ״כאשר ידבר איש אל רעהו״ — this one speaks and that one answers, without any intermediary, and he was not bewildered at all. And this excellence is one of the excellences that Moshe possessed in his prophecy, through which he surpassed all the other prophets, and through it he was exalted and lifted above them all, elevated and borne on high exceedingly; and it is that which is called knowledge face to face. And so Scripture testified concerning him at the end of the Torah (Deuteronomy 34:10): ״ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה אשר ידעו ה׳ פנים אל פנים״. And concerning this excellence the prophet promised those who fear YHWH and think upon His name that they will attain it in the world that is entirely long, as it is said (Isaiah 33:17): ״מלך ביופיו תחזינה עיניך תראינה ארץ מרחקים״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:30.704765874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}